From: devvm <https://github.com/bindreams/devvm>
Subject: [PATCH] target/i386/whpx: do not advertise the enlightened NPT TLB

With nested virtualization on, the hypervisor answers CPUID 0x4000000A for a WHP
partition with the enlightened NPT TLB bit (EAX bit 22) set, but it rejects the
two hypercalls that enlightenment consists of: HvCallFlushGuestPhysicalAddressSpace
and HvCallFlushGuestPhysicalAddressList return HV_STATUS_INVALID_HYPERCALL_CODE.
A guest hypervisor that turns the enlightenment on promises to flush by hypercall
and cannot, so the hypervisor keeps running its guests on stale nested page
tables. A Windows guest with the Windows Hypervisor Platform then hands out a
hypercall page whose overlay its own guests do not see.

Measured on AMD. On Intel the same two hypercalls are advertised by EAX bit 18
(HV_X64_NESTED_GUEST_MAPPING_FLUSH); that was not measured and is left as the
hypervisor reports it. Intercept the leaf and clear the bit.

To be upstreamed; applies after 0003.
---
--- a/target/i386/kvm/hyperv-proto.h
+++ b/target/i386/kvm/hyperv-proto.h
@@ -92,6 +92,7 @@
  */
 #define HV_NESTED_DIRECT_FLUSH              (1u << 17)
 #define HV_NESTED_MSR_BITMAP                (1u << 19)
+#define HV_NESTED_ENLIGHTENED_TLB           (1u << 22)
 
 /*
  * Basic virtualized MSRs
--- a/target/i386/whpx/whpx-all.c
+++ b/target/i386/whpx/whpx-all.c
@@ -2619,6 +2619,21 @@
                 }
             }
 
+            /*
+             * On AMD hosts the hypervisor advertises the enlightened NPT TLB
+             * to a WHP partition but rejects the hypercalls it consists of
+             * (HvCallFlushGuestPhysicalAddressSpace and ...List), so a guest
+             * hypervisor that turns it on runs its guests on stale nested page
+             * tables. Answer as the hypervisor does, without that bit.
+             */
+            if ((uint32_t)vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rax == HV_CPUID_NESTED_FEATURES) {
+                reg_values[1].Reg64 = vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.DefaultResultRax;
+                reg_values[2].Reg64 = vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.DefaultResultRcx;
+                reg_values[3].Reg64 = vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.DefaultResultRdx;
+                reg_values[4].Reg64 = vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.DefaultResultRbx;
+                reg_values[1].Reg64 &= ~(uint64_t)HV_NESTED_ENLIGHTENED_TLB;
+            }
+
             if (vcpu->exit_ctx.CpuidAccess.Rax == 0x1) {
                 if (cpu_has_x2apic_feature(env)) {
                     reg_values[2].Reg64 |= CPUID_EXT_X2APIC;
@@ -3085,7 +3100,8 @@
     WHV_PROCESSOR_PERFMON_FEATURES perfmon_features;
 
     UINT32 cpuidExitList[] = {0x0, 0x1, 0x6, 0x7, 0xb, 0xd, 0x14, 0x24, 0x29, 0x1E,
-        0x40000000, 0x40000001, 0x40000010, 0x80000000, 0x80000001,
+        0x40000000, 0x40000001, HV_CPUID_NESTED_FEATURES, 0x40000010,
+        0x80000000, 0x80000001,
         0x80000002, 0x80000003, 0x80000004, 0x80000007, 0x80000008,
         0x8000000A, 0x80000021, 0x80000022, 0xC0000000, 0xC0000001};
 
